<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="33160" href="schedule/2007/spring/EALC/398/33160.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2007" href="schedule/2007.xml">2007</calendarYear><term id="120071" href="schedule/2007/spring.xml">Spring 2007</term><subject id="EALC" href="schedule/2007/spring/EALC.xml">East Asian Language and Culture</subject><course id="398" href="schedule/2007/spring/EALC/398.xml">Colloquium in EALC</course></parents><sectionNumber>C  </sectionNumber><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Reading Japanese History Through Fiction In this course, we will read about ten works of Japanese "historical"  fiction and drama that retell specific historical events from the  sixteenth to twentieth century. We will think about how to interpret  historical events, how those events are narrated, and how we understand them as history. Supplementary readings will help us guide our interpretive methods for reading narrative structures.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2007-01-16Z</startDate><endDate>2007-05-02Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>10:00 AM</start><end>11:20 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1048</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Toyosawa" firstName="N">Toyosawa, N</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>